WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:06.540
Operant conditioning is based on the
idea that we can increase or decrease a

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:13.380
certain behavior by adding a consequence.
For example if a dog poops on a carpet

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.090
we can either provide reinforcement so
the dog does it again or punishments of

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:23.310
the dog stops. Both reinforcement and
punishment can either be positive or

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:30.140
negative, which means we have four
possible ways to teach this dog a lesson.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:36.540
We can draw the four options in a table:
if reinforcement is positive we add

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:41.180
something pleasant like a cookie to
increase the likelihood of a behavior. If

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:46.680
reinforcement is negative we still want
to increase the desired behavior this

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:52.020
time by removing something unpleasant
like the leash. If punishment is positive

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.760
we add an unpleasant response to
decrease behavior. When punishment is

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.410
negative we also want to decrease
behavior now by removing something

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:06.210
pleasant like the comfy carpet. If we
stop any sort of manipulation the

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.729
conditioned behavior will eventually
disappear again.

00:01:08.729 --> 00:01:15.960
This is called extinction. Operant
conditioning was first studied by Edward

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:21.720
L. Thorndike and later made famous by the
work of B.F. Skinner. Skinner believed that

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.470
organisms are doing what they do
naturally until they accidentally

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.310
encounter a stimulus that creates
conditioning which results in a change

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:34.979
in behavior. To test this he placed a rat
inside an operant conditioning chamber

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:40.890
which later became known as the Skinner
Box. Among other things inside the box

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:47.759
was a lever that would release food when
pressed. Conditioning happens in a

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:54.060
three-term contingency today known as
the ABCs of behavior. A stands for

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:59.219
antecedent: the rat accidentally hits the
lever that triggers the release of food.

00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:05.340
B stands for behavior and refers to the
response: the rat keeps pressing the

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:10.959
lever. C stands for consequence: food
keeps coming out.

00:02:10.959 --> 00:02:14.829
The strength of the response to the
conditioning depends on the schedule of

00:02:14.829 --> 00:02:19.540
reinforcement. If there is always food
after pressing, the rat behaves

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:24.939
predictably. If the food is released
randomly the rat behaves erratically,

00:02:24.939 --> 00:02:31.329
like an addict. Skinner born in 1904 was
a professor of psychology and subscribed

00:02:31.329 --> 00:02:36.129
to behaviorism. He argued that you can
only study behavior that is visible and

00:02:36.129 --> 00:02:41.019
anything happening only within the mind
is either a misconception or irrelevant

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:44.349
to science.
He thought free will was an illusion

00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:48.489
because behavior is either random or a
reaction to the environment. His work

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:53.169
became the foundation for behavioral
therapy, military drills, and animal

00:02:53.169 --> 00:03:00.430
training. You can try this classroom
exercise on positive reinforcement: One

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:06.010
individual must exit the room. Now decide
on a task which that individual will

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:11.620
complete, such as finding a particular
book. Then choose an honorable way of

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:16.060
reinforcing that tasks such as clapping
your hands. Invite the person to come

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:20.349
back into the room and let them try and
complete the task but don't give any

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:24.370
instructions. Every time they are on the
right track in regards to completing the

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:29.290
task clap your hands louder. If they move
away from performing the task reduce

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:33.340
your applause or stop it entirely. Once
the person understands what they are

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:38.939
supposed to do let them explain the task.
Did they get it right?

